    desc: "In northern New Ireland the malanggan cycle honours the dead with carved and painted figures, friezes and great ceremonial gates, commissioned years after death and unveiled at a final mortuary feast. Motifs — raptor beaks, fish fins, spiralling openwork blades, the bulging ternate head — belong to specific clans who borrow, buy and trade designs like property; a carver works from dreams and payment, never free imitation. At the climax the carving is danced, shown once, and then abandoned in the bush to decay, because its power is spent. Missionaries suppressed the rites, yet malanggan never died: festivals resumed around Kavieng and Libba, and 19th-century collections in Berlin, Dresden and Sydney made the style one of the most studied sculptural systems of the Pacific.",
