    desc: "The kula is the Massim region's great ceremonial exchange: necklaces (soulava or bagi) travel clockwise between islands while Conus shell armbands (mwali) travel counter-clockwise, each valuable paired with a permanent partner and held only temporarily before passing on. The objects themselves are superb craft — ground and polished white Conus armshells with turtle-shell lashing, and necklaces of hundreds of small red Spondylus and banana-orange shell discs graded by fineness. Every famous valuable carries a name, an owner list and a biography; fame accrues to shells that circulate widely. Malinowski's Argonauts of the Western Pacific (1922) made kula the founding case study of economic anthropology, and the exchanges continue today around the Trobriands, Woodlark and Amphlett islands, now with motor canoes beside the ornate lagim-prowed sailing craft.",
