    desc: "Tevau, the feather money of the Santa Cruz group, is among the most labour-intensive currencies ever made: each roll consists of a central spine carrying ten-foot bands of small rounded plates, each plate a compacted turtle-shell or wood disc layered with dozens of iridescent red feathers from the cardinal lory, plucked with tiny sections of skin and glued in shingled rows. A single coil took makers on Taumako hundreds of bird skins and months of work, graded from new red to sun-faded yellowish with age. Used principally as bride-wealth, tevau circulated in strict regional accounts and was largely abandoned after the 1930s as trade money flooded in. Smithsonian, Cambridge and museum collections preserve the great coils, and their shingled crimson texture has become an icon of Pacific virtuoso craft.",
