    desc: "On the Tiwi Islands, death concludes when carved ironwood poles — pukumani — are erected around the grave and left to decay with the deceased's possessions. Carvers work commissioned by the mourning family, sculpting tapering posts that may end in anthropomorphic heads, birds or abstract finials, then covering them in jilamara: the striped, dotted and banded body-paint designs that are the core of Tiwi visual identity, applied in white pipeclay, red and yellow ochre and black. Because every design is individually invented for the mourner and the dead, no two pole groups repeat, and Tiwi painting today — on canvas, bark, ironwood birds and screen-printed cloth — grew directly from this funerary grammar. Museum suites from Bathurst Island, and the continuing workshops of Nguiu (Wurrumiyanga) and Milikapiti, keep the tradition emphatically alive.",
