    sum: "Ghost gums, mauve ranges and pale desert light in watercolour — the Central Australian movement Albert Namatjira founded.",
    desc: "When Victorian watercolourist Rex Battarbee exhibited at Hermannsburg Lutheran mission in 1934, Western Arrernte artist Albert Namatjira took up the medium with immediate brilliance; Battarbee tutored him, and by 1938 Namatjira's first solo show in Melbourne sold out. The Hermannsburg style renders Central Australia — ghost gums, the MacDonnell Ranges, salt-soaked creek beds — in luminous washes of mauve, ultramarine and warm earth, with sharp leafless gums as the signature motif, coupling European landscape technique with Arrernte country knowledge and totemic attachment to place. Namatjira's fame (and 1957 citizenship, and 1959 imprisonment controversy) made the school a national story; relatives and successors such as the Pareroultja brothers and the Hermannsburg Potters continue at Ntaria, and Araluen Arts Centre's Namatjira exhibitions anchor the movement's legacy.",
