    sum: "Giant limestone discs up to four metres across, quarried in Palau and valued by history rather than size.",
    desc: "Rai — the stone money of Yap — are calcite-limestone discs with a central hole, ranging from dinner-plate size to monsters over three tonnes, quarried with shell and later iron tools in Palau's Rock Islands and transported hundreds of kilometres on outrigger rafts. Their value is not weight but biography: the peril of the voyage, the fame of the cutter, the number of men who died, and an unbroken oral ledger of ownership. Ownership transfers without movement — a disc at the bottom of a channel, lost in the 1800s when a storm sank a raft, is still owned and spent — a fact economists love to cite against pure gold-standard thinking. German, Japanese and American administrations left rai standing; the discs still flank stone-money banks and paths and village meeting places across Yap; state law and community custodianship now protect the quarry sites and discs.",
