    sum: "Nearly a hundred artificial islets walled in columnar basalt logs — a stone city on a reef off Pohnpei.",
    desc: "Nan Madol, the ceremonial and political seat of the Saudeleur dynasty, spreads across 90-plus artificial islets built on a reef flat off Temwen Island, Pohnpei, linked by tidal canals that earned it the label 'Venice of the Pacific'. Its walls are laid in header-and-stretcher courses of prismatic columnar basalt — dark, log-like prisms quarried far away and stacked up to 8 metres high without mortar — enclosing basalt cobble platforms, tombs such as Nandauwas, and elite compounds. Archaeology dates the megalithic building surge to roughly 1180-1200 CE, with occupation on the site earlier still; oral history credits the twin sorcerer-brothers Olosihpa and Olosohpa with levitating the stones. Abandoned after the Saudeleur's fall, Nan Madol was inscribed by UNESCO in 2016 (World Heritage in Danger) and remains Pacific archaeology's signature megalithic enigma.",
