    cat: "Religious & Sacred Art", c: "monolithic ancestor statuary", e: "regional_tradition", o: "Rapa Nui (Easter Island) carvers", g: "Rapa Nui, Chile (Polynesia)",
    p: "c. 1250-1500 CE carving peak", era: "classic megalithic",
    sum: "Nearly 900 colossal ancestor figures carved from Rano Raraku tuff, standing ahu platforms along the Rapa Nui coast.",
    desc: "The moai are the Pacific's most recognisable sculpture: monolithic figures with heavy brow, elongated ears and clenched lips, carved in the compressed volcanic tuff of Rano Raraku quarry and moved — by teams, tracks and trees, the transport method still argued — to ahu platforms ringing the island. Most statues face inland to watch over their communities rather than the sea, and about one hundred wear pukao topknots of red scoria from Puna Pau; late-era ahu like Tongariki line up fifteen. Coral-and-obsidian eyes were inserted only for ritual activation, as the restored moai at Tahai shows, and the 18th-century collapse of the statue cult gave way to the birdman (tangata manu) ceremony at Orongo. UNESCO-listed since 1995, the moai anchor living Rapa Nui identity — and ongoing repatriation debates over figures like Hoa Hakananai'a in the British Museum.",
    col: [{ h: "#8d8578", n: "tuff grey-beige" }, { h: "#9c4a2b", n: "pukao red scoria" }, { h: "#3d6b6e", n: "Pacific teal" }, { h: "#5d7a4e", n: "sloping grass green" }, { h: "#2b2622", n: "quarry shadow" }],
