    p: "surviving tablets mostly 18th-19th c; script older", era: "undeciphered writing",
    sum: "Rows of incised bird, fish and figure glyphs on wooden tablets — one of the world's few independent writing systems, still unread.",
    desc: "Rongorongo is the undeciphered script of Rapa Nui: incised rows of pictographic signs — birdmen, turtles, dancing figures, stars — carved with obsidian flakes and shark teeth on wooden tablets (kohau), paddles and staffs. The tablets are read in reverse boustrophedon, each line turned 180 degrees from the last, and fewer than thirty objects survive worldwide, in museums from Saint Petersburg to Honolulu; most were collected in the 1860s-70s after missionisation and slave raids broke the learned tradition. Attempts to crack the code have yielded partial wins — the Mamari tablet's sequence is widely read as a lunar calendar, and repetition patterns suggest a genuine writing system rather than memory art — but the corpus is too small and too few texts for full decipherment. UNESCO's Memory of the World registers the corpus, which remains the Pacific's greatest linguistic puzzle.",
    col: [{ h: "#3a2c20", n: "tablet brown" }, { h: "#d9c9a3", n: "incised ochre dust" }, { h: "#1f1a14", n: "charcoal groove" }, { h: "#a3752f", n: "red-ochre rub" }, { h: "#6e7f6a", n: "specimen-case sage" }],
