    cat: "Fashion & Dress", c: "full-body blackwork tattoo", e: "regional_tradition", o: "Marquesan (Enana/Enata) tattoo masters", g: "Marquesas Islands, French Polynesia",
    p: "pre-contact origin; revived from 1980s", era: "revived blackwork classic",
    sum: "Dense blackwork of tiki, enata figures and lizards covering Marquesan bodies — the tattoo tradition that seeded sailor tattooing.",
    desc: "Marquesan tattooing (te patu tiki, 'to tap the figure') was among the most extensive in Polynesia: by adulthood, warriors bore head-to-ankle blackwork — dense bands, spirals, lizard and tiki figures, rows of stylised enata humans — tapped in with bone combs (au) and mallets, soot-mixed with candlenut soot and coconut oil. Early European voyagers were stunned; the Marquesan repertoire, filtered through sailor skin, fed the very genesis of Western tattooing, and Marquesan crosses and spearheads remain staples of global tattoo flash. Missionaries all but ended the practice by the late 19th century, but the 1980s revival led by artists like Teve Tupu and Tahiti's tatau movement restored hand-tapping in the Marquesas, where Motu Haka federation festivals now showcase full-body work rooted in documented 19th-century designs collected by Karl von den Steinen.",
