    n: "Samoan tatau", a: ["pe'a full-body tattoo", "malu thigh tattoo", "tufuga tā tatau tradition"],
    cat: "Fashion & Dress", c: "pe'a and malu body tattoo", e: "regional_tradition", o: "tufuga ta tavau tattoo families (Sulu'ape lineage)", g: "Samoa (and Samoan diaspora)",
    p: "pre-contact; near-extinct c. 1900-1970s; strong revival since 1980s", era: "revived living tradition",
    sum: "The pe'a and malu — dense bottleneck-to-knee blackwork tapped by hereditary tufuga, worn as service and identity.",
    desc: "Samoan tatau is the Pacific's most unbroken tattoo tradition: the male pe'a covers from waist to knees in dense blackwork — bands, triangles and va'a canoe motifs — while women may receive the malu on the thighs, finer and more sparse. Work is performed by hereditary masters, the tufuga ta tavau, whose Sulu'ape lineage runs an 'au group of assistants and hand-taps ink of burnt lama-nut soot with carved bone and turtle-shell combs over days or weeks; the recipient is called a soga'imiti, carrying pain as service to family and chief. The legend holds that tattooing was brought from Fiji by two women whose song garbled the words, giving Samoa the tattoo and Fiji the woodpecker. After mission-era decline, the 1980s-90s revival — cemented by Su'a Sulu'ape Petelo and films like Tatau (1980) — made tatau a proud marker of Samoan identity worldwide.",
