    sum: "Crescent feather headdresses — turtle-shell arms, white down, eagle plumes — worn in Torres Strait dance and set on the islanders' flag.",
    desc: "The dhari (also dari) is the ceremonial headdress of the Torres Strait Islands: a crescent halo of carved and painted turtle shell or modern card, edged with white feathers, cockatoo down and sweeping eagle or frigate-bird plumes, worn by male dancers to catch motion during martial and celebratory island dances. Regional styles vary island by island, and 19th-century examples collected by missionaries — and Haddon's landmark 1898 Cambridge Anthropological Expedition to the Torres Strait — record elaborate sculpted arms with figures, fish and frigate birds. Mission strictures nearly stopped the great headdresses, but the dhari became the central emblem of the Torres Strait Islander Flag (adopted 1992, designed by the late Bernard Namok), and festivals on Thursday Island, Masig and Erub float new dhari made with paper, paint and feathers above island dance troupes.",
