    cat: "Textile & Craft", c: "looped (non-woven) string bags", e: "regional_tradition", o: "Papua New Guinean women weavers", g: "Papua New Guinea, especially Highlands and Sepik",
    p: "pre-contact to present", era: "living everyday textile",
    sum: "Looped, knotless string bags — bilum — carrying babies, food and status, woven one continuous strand at a time across PNG.",
    desc: "The bilum is Papua New Guinea's national bag and its most intimate textile: made without loom or knot by looping a single continuous strand — traditionally bark string, orchid stem or possum fur, today often acrylic — into an open mesh that stretches to carry garden produce, firewood, babies and, in ceremonial versions, shell valuables. Weavers suspend the work from a foot or post and build each bag spirally, working patterns in as they go; colours and designs signal clan, region and occasion, from the white-and-ochre Sepik bilum to the brilliant acrylic brights of the Highlands. Bilum weaving is women's work passed from mother to daughter, and the 2010s saw bilum lines walk Pacific fashion runways and a bilum weavers' association drive fair-trade orders — a heritage craft now formally listed among PNG's protected cultural expressions.",
    col: [{ h: "#e8e0d0", n: "bark string cream" }, { h: "#c23b4e", n: "acrylic red" }, { h: "#2e6f5e", n: "highland green" }, { h: "#e8a020", n: "banara yellow" }, { h: "#3a3a3c", n: "charcoal twist" }],
