    cat: "Architectural Style", c: "towering ceremonial house architecture", e: "regional_tradition", o: "Abelam, Kwoma and related Sepik peoples", g: "East Sepik and border provinces, Papua New Guinea",
    p: "documented 20th c; far older origins", era: "living ceremonial architecture",
    sum: "A-frame ceremonial houses rising up to twenty metres, their gable façades painted with ancestor faces in orange, black and white.",
    desc: "The haus tambaran is the great men's house of the Sepik region: an A-frame shelter whose ridge may soar 20-25 metres in the Maprik-Abelam style, sheathed in sago thatch and fronted by a vast painted gable — a stacked face-and-body diagram of ancestors and spirits rendered in the region's four-colour code of black, white, red and yellow-orange ochre. Inside, flutes, drums and carved figures embody the tambaran spirits shown to initiates; the house itself is the cult, rebuilt in competition between clans and forbidden to uninitiated women. Kwoma and Iatmul variants lower the ridge and shift decoration to finials and interior rafters of painted sago-palm spathe. Mission-era suppression was partial and temporary; tambaran houses still anchor village ceremony along the Sepik and around Maprik, echoed in the Papua New Guinea parliament's 1992 permanent haus tambaran chamber in Waigani.",
    col: [{ h: "#c8781e", n: "gable ochre" }, { h: "#2a211a", n: "thatch brown-black" }, { h: "#f0e6d0", n: "lime white" }, { h: "#1d1814", n: "soot black" }, { h: "#7d8a4f", n: "sago-leaf green" }],
