    sum: "Polished sperm-whale teeth, strung on braided sennit, presented to bind weddings, funerals, wars and chiefly requests.",
    desc: "The tabua — a polished sperm-whale tooth strung on plaited coconut sennit and anointed with coconut oil and red ochre — is Fiji's supreme ceremonial valuable, presented (vakarokoroko, with formal speech) at weddings, funerals, peacemaking, installation of chiefs and requests for favour. Teeth originally came from stranded whales and Tongan exchange, then from 19th-century trading ships and American whalers; value scaled with size, age and patina, and a great tabua could settle a war. Each tooth has a name and story; presentations follow strict sevusevu etiquette still observed today in Methodist weddings and chiefly functions. Museums from the Fiji Museum to the Metropolitan hold major tabua, and the term defines Fijian prestige exchange as kula does for the Trobriands — wealth whose worth lies in relationships, not material.",
