    sum: "Carved ancestral figures crowning Kanak grand-case roofs — the flèche faîtière, now the emblem of Kanak identity.",
    desc: "At the roof-peak of the Kanak grand case — the circular thatched chief's house — rises the flèche faîtière: a carved central spear whose stacked, flex-legged ancestor figures with pierced, heart-shaped faces spear upward through thatch, anchoring the lineage to its land and mediating between clan, ancestors and the spirits above. Collected examples date to the 18th century (La Pérouse's expedition took one in 1788, now in the British Museum), and colonial suppression of grand-case building nearly ended the practice. Revival came through customary rebuilding and Jean-Marie Tjibaou's cultural renaissance: Renzo Piano's 1998 Jean-Marie Tjibaou Cultural Centre abstracts the flèche into great iroko 'cases', and the Kanak flag carries the flèche silhouette inside its yellow disc. Carvers today produce flèche for clan houses, festivals and diplomacy — New Caledonia's most legible art symbol.",
