    sum: "Crimson and gold feather capes and crested helmets — the supreme regalia of the Hawaiian ali'i, woven feather by feather.",
    desc: "ʻAhuʻula — literally 'red garments' — are the feathered capes of Hawaiian high chiefs, built by tying bundles of tiny feathers into olonā-fibre netting shaped to the body: tens of thousands of red feathers from the ʻiʻiwi and ʻapapane honeycreepers and rare yellow from the ʻōʻō and mamo, whose two feathers per bird made a yellow cloak worth thousands of birds and years of specialist labour. Matching mahiole helmets carried the same feather crests; designs ranged from plain crimson fields to gold crescents and crescent-field compositions. The tradition culminated in Kamehameha I's legendary all-yellow cape, and in the vast pāʻū cloak of Princess Nāhienaena (c. 1830s), the Bishop Museum's treasure. European collecting dispersed ʻahuʻula worldwide — one of the 'Cook' capes sits in the British Museum — while Hawai'i institutions repatriate and revere the survivors as ancestors in feather.",
