    sum: "Big-headed, jaw-forward figures lashed to canoe prows — nguzunguzu charms that shielded bonito fishers from sea spirits.",
    desc: "Nguzunguzu (also nguzu nguzu, toto isu) are the carved figureheads of the ornate tomoko war and bonito canoes of New Georgia and Roviana Lagoon, Solomon Islands: compact figures with oversized heads, wide frontal jaws, hands raised to chin or head, and eyes of inlaid shell, lashed facing forward at the waterline where spirit and wave meet. Their role was protective — guarding crews against the formidable ocean spirits that governed bonito runs, and the bonito itself was sacred, caught by lure from special platforms and presented in rituals. Missionisation and the 20th-century decline of canoe building ended most prow carving, but museum corpora (British Museum, Metropolitan, Auckland) preserve the distinctive silhouette, and the form survives in contemporary Solomon Islander carving, logo design and the bone-carving revival around Roviana and Marovo lagoons.",
